Fedora 42 Workstation Installation Process
Boot into Fedora 42 using the Live ISO.

Start the installer and select the language and keyboard layout.

Select the available partition.

Open the three-dot menu and select Launch Storage Editor.

Create free space on the disk by shrinking the existing partition.

Linux requires approximately 40 GB of free space.

Install the setup and finish the installation.

Dual-Boot Fedora and Windows
Preparation:

Back up important Windows data.

Use Windows Disk Management to shrink the existing Windows partition.

Create at least 40 GB of unallocated space.

Download the Fedora Workstation 42 ISO.

Create a bootable USB using Rufus or Fedora Media Writer.

Set the partition scheme to GPT.

Installation:

Boot the computer from the Fedora USB.

Start the installation.

Select language and keyboard layout.

Select the unallocated space as the installation destination.

Choose automatic or custom partitioning.

For custom partitioning, create /boot/efi, /boot and / partitions.

Optionally create a /home partition.

Enable encryption if required.

Review settings and click Begin Installation.

Complete the final setup.

Dual-Boot Configuration:

Fedora configures the GRUB bootloader to choose between Windows and Fedora.

BIOS/UEFI boot order may need to be adjusted.

Important Considerations:

Secure Boot may cause installation or booting issues.

Carefully review partitioning to avoid deleting Windows.

Consult Fedora documentation or the Fedora community for troubleshooting.

Conclusion:
In this way, we successfully install Fedora 42 Workstation on a personal computer.
